Files / mechanisms
src/process.rs:45 reserve_group allocates a slot BEFORE spawning. All tools,
their Terminal projections, key-command resolvers and MCP servers use the same
ownership mechanism. The fixed 256-slot registry never evicts: exhaustion fails
before spawn. Paired SeqCst publication/closing loads fence a concurrent spawn.
:97 kill_owned_groups is a synchronous bounded scan of lock-free atomics and
kill(-pgid,SIGKILL). It has no allocation, mutex, runtime task, IO wait or await.
:113 shutdown_signal preserves errno; :126 installs SIGHUP/SIGTERM handlers
before Tokio registers its chained listeners. Thus group SIGKILL dispatch happens
on the signal path, before async cancellation/cleanup is notified. PTY EOF/EIO
calls the same function before Close or any await (headless.rs:350).
The 100ms dispatch budget is implemented by this direct signal path; the model
abstracts time, and the process witness uses the specified 250ms escalation and
2s ESRCH bound.
Registration retirement claims the atomic slot against an in-flight signal and
removes it BEFORE child.wait can release/reuse the PID. WNOWAIT ownership and
normal TERM/grace/KILL cancellation remain. Unit witnesses check no signal after
retirement and idempotence after slot reuse.
src/mcp.rs:486 finish_child owns the same group guard from spawn through
TERM/one-second grace/KILL/retirement/reap. :574 reserves before SDK spawn and
binds immediately, without awaiting handshake.
src/frontends/headless.rs distinguishes a healthy cooked Ctrl-D from a hung-up
PTY via poll/termios. Ctrl-D stays graceful: Close, joined groups, SessionClosed,
fsync, exit0. A seat's prior failed turn does not change the successful Close
exit status; one-shot headless still reports its turn failure.
:296 resume first acquires Host::open's exclusive writer lease, then removes
only an owned stale socket (never a symlink or other file) before binding. SIGKILL
leaves that pathname behind; the real recovery witness exposed this defect.

Process evidence
tests/seat_process.rs includes tests/support/seat_shutdown.rs.
:155 supervisor_case runs a real PTY, a scripted bash tool, and a real fixture
MCP server plus member. The tool ignores both HUP and TERM, as required by the
Oracle/E4 discriminator correction 01M6BNHCXY9B4W50H3Q7TFBZEK:
trap '' HUP; trap '' TERM; ...; sleep 600.
This prevents the ordinary 500ms cancel grace from accidentally passing the
shorter 250ms supervisor budget.
SIGHUP, SIGTERM and actual last-master-close each escalate to harness SIGKILL
after250ms. All owned groups must reach ESRCH within2s. The killed exit is
recorded, not asserted. Every case then runs seat --resume, observes
SessionRecovered, closes with Ctrl-D, and runs check_transcript over the log.
:254 Ctrl-D at an idle prompt exits0 within2s, ends the log in SessionClosed and
leaves no MCP group. The fixture cleans its own groups even under a broken
production mutation. Exit waits are bounded; no operator or physical probe.
pty-cases.log records three actual ExitStatus{code:1,signal:Some("Killed: 9")}
observations and all four passing cases.
During test development the exit observation was moved after the orphan check:
Darwin can hold a killed leader in exit while orphaned holders remain. ESRCH
polling tolerates intermediate kernel results. These are fixture corrections,
not relaxed exit/group assertions. The stale socket recovery failure was fixed
in the product; no test removes that socket.

